{
  "dom_id": "step-q160789",
  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)=\u27e8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+s),\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-s),\\,\\frac{1}{1+s}\u27e9.$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components. \n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1+s)$ is $s\\in (-1,\\infty )$ .\n\n- The domain of $\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(1-s)$ is $s\\in (-\\infty ,1).$\n\n- The domain of $\\frac{1}{1+s}$ is  $s\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033854/assets/3ac2933afc460e42ad5bffb8b01b9fc3ff9275ab3bcf2ac3ab710a8094b36637.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(s)$ is\n\n$\\,$ $s\\in (-1,1).$",
